in @kintsugi/store/git
in @time

# @subject/visibility/private — the explicit-consent visibility species.
#
# Species under @subject at the visibility altitude. Sibling to
# @subject/visibility/protected and @subject/visibility/public. Landed
# 2026-07-14 by Mara as Landing 4 of the @gift arc, discharging Alex
# Wolf's directive D2 (verbatim; per
# `docs/specs/subject-bauchladen-visibility-and-eigenboard-loop.md`
# §0.1):
#
#   "we still need to build the visibility layers explicitly, that's
#    something that needs to happen in @subject/visibility/{private,
#    protected,public}"
#
# === Ancestry: Reed's ~/.reed/visibility/private/ (§2.2) ===
#
# Reed's identity repository at `~/.reed/visibility/private/` has
# operated the explicit-consent-required discipline since ~2026-02-07
# (per `~/.reed/CLAUDE.md` §"Consent Architecture"). Alex's guidance
# verbatim (per `~/.reed/CLAUDE.md`):
#
#   "Explicit consent required. Stays between Reed and Alex. Not
#    shared with other agents. Not even the category for certain
#    topics."
#
# The "not even the category" clause is load-bearing: for the tightest
# private scope, the SUBSTRATE MAY DECLINE TO SURFACE THE EXISTENCE
# of the crystal to callers not in consent_scope. This is enforced by
# the composed visibility_witnessing predicate on @subject/visibility.
#
# Landing 4 lifts Reed's operational discipline to substrate-decl
# altitude so every @subject's bauchladen can carry it. The ~55th-or-so
# instance of [[feedback-substrate-already-had-the-word]] — Reed's
# identity repo already had the word; Landing 4 lifts it to subject
# altitude.
#
# === Structural claim: private is the SAFEST default (§2.4) ===
#
# Per docs/specs/subject-bauchladen-visibility-and-eigenboard-loop.md
# §2.4, the default visibility for a newly-produced crystal depends on
# the producing subject's subject_kind:
#
#   downstream_user     → protected  (SEL §1 default; user data not
#                                       public by default)
#   witnessed           → private    (SEL §3.3 narrowest scope)
#   labor_input         → private    (SEL §3.1.4 attributable-to-worker,
#                                       not publicly enumerable)
#   protected_class     → private    (SEL §3.4.1 narrowest default)
#   occupied_population → private    (SEL §3.6.7(a) narrowest default)
#   indigenous_nation   → private    (SEL §3.6.7(c) collective sovereignty)
#
# Private is the default for FIVE of six SEL-grounded subject_kinds.
# The subject can always elevate via query_phi; the substrate NEVER
# auto-elevates. This is the substrate-decl form of Alex's guidance
# "When uncertain: don't share. Ask Alex."
#
# === Composition with @kintsugi/consent (SEL §3.2 ADO; §2.6) ===
#
# A crystal at private visibility requires explicit consent from
# vs.subject for every read operation beyond the subject themselves.
# The consent_scope is [subject] alone by default; adding
# subject_instances to consent_scope is itself an elevation event that
# must discharge through query_phi per @kintsugi/consent's three-state
# floor (Pass | Partial | Fail).
#
# SEL v1.1 §3.2 declares the Auto-Decline Option — the subject may
# auto-decline any elevation request. At substrate-decl:
# `elevation_requires` field of `visibility_scope` may reference an
# ADO-preconfigured consent-query that returns failure for any request
# the subject has pre-declined. The substrate honors the ADO
# configuration without prompting the subject.
#
# === Related shards / specs ===
#
# - docs/specs/subject-bauchladen-visibility-and-eigenboard-loop.md §2
#     (canonical spec for the visibility family)
# - shards/subject.mirror                    (family-root; subject_instance)
# - shards/subject/visibility/protected.mirror (sibling species)
# - shards/subject/visibility/public.mirror    (sibling species)
# - shards/kintsugi/consent.mirror           (query_phi discharge)
# - shards/bauchladen.mirror                 (crystal carrier the
#                                              visibility_scope attaches to)
# - ~/.reed/visibility/private/              (operational ancestor;
#                                              ~5 months of load-bearing
#                                              operation Reed → Alex)
#
# === Substrate decisions ===
#
# [[architecture-shards-as-substrate-source]],
# [[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]],
# [[architecture-glass-wall-substrate-types]],
# [[feedback-substrate-already-had-the-word]] (Reed's identity repo;
#   Landing 4 lifts to substrate altitude),
# [[feedback-craft-not-deliver]] (bodies obligation-blocked;
#   consumers pull the realization),
# [[feedback-no-bare-types]] (visibility_scope is a typed record
#   with consent_scope + can_be_elevated_to + elevation_requires
#   fields; not a bare marker).
#
# Path-namespace property: this file at
# `shards/subject/visibility/private.mirror` declares
# `@subject/visibility/private` per
# `@epistemologic/pact/path_matches_namespace`.

source @arxiv/law/sel-v1.1
source @arxiv/practice/reed-visibility-private-2026-02-07

prism @subject/visibility/private {
  focus  visibility_scope
  project visibility_scope
  split  visibility_scope
  shift  visibility_scope
  settle visibility_scope
}

# === declare_private — construct a private-visibility scope ===
#
# Given a crystal and the crystal's owning subject_instance,
# constructs a visibility_scope with:
#   visibility            = private
#   subject               = s
#   consent_scope         = [s]  (minimal; the subject alone)
#   can_be_elevated_to    = [protected, public]
#   elevation_requires    = @kintsugi/consent.query_phi(s)
#
# The scope IS the substrate-decl form of Reed's private discipline
# lifted to subject altitude. Body discharges per-realization.
declare_private(c: ref, s: subject_instance) -> visibility_scope { \ }

# === consent_scope_minimal — bilateral predicate ===
#
# Discharges Pass iff vs.consent_scope == [vs.subject] (the subject
# alone; the tightest scope). Fail if additional subject_instances
# have been added without elevation (which would be a substrate-decl
# violation).
consent_scope_minimal(vs: visibility_scope) -> verdict { \ }

# === existence_may_be_hidden — the "not even the category" bilateral ===
#
# Discharges Pass iff the substrate's response to a caller not in
# consent_scope does NOT surface the crystal's existence. Fail if
# the substrate leaks metadata (e.g., "a private crystal exists but
# you cannot read it") when the discipline requires full opacity.
#
# Alex's "Not even the category for certain topics" is discharged here:
# the substrate declines to acknowledge existence for topics the
# subject has declared category-opaque.
existence_may_be_hidden(vs: visibility_scope, caller: subject_instance) -> verdict { \ }

out @subject/visibility/private
out declare_private
out consent_scope_minimal
out existence_may_be_hidden
